rom irrelevant or overly localized patterns caused by rare instances.\n\n# Explanation why the column nCRX3 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nCRX3'], inplace=True)\n# nCRX3: Number of CRX3 is another sparse feature (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0363, Std = 0.2457) that does not contribute significantly to the chemical composition or biodegradation prediction.\n# Usefulness: Eliminates a descriptor that provides negligible variation or learning signal to the model.\n\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n# B04[C-Br]: Presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 4 is sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0253, Std = 0.1571) and does not reflect broader molecular descriptors useful for biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: Reduces redundancy caused by sparse categorical variables that contribute little to the task.\nend", "python\n# Step 1: Generate additional interaction features\n# Interaction between nHDon (number of donor atoms for H-bonds) and nO (Number of oxygen atoms)\ndf['nHDon_nO_interaction'] = df['nHDon'] * df['nO']\n# Feature description: Captures interaction between donor atoms for hydrogen bonds and the number of oxygen atoms, which are both critical in biochemical reactions.\n# Usefulness: This reflects the potential for a molecule to engage in hydrogen bonding, which is relevant for biodegradation prediction.\n\n# Interaction between nCb- (number of substituted benzene C(sp2)) and F03[C-N] (frequency of C-N at topological distance 3)\ndf['nCb-_F03[C-N]_interaction'] = df['nCb-'] * df['F03[C-N]']\n# Feature description: Shows the relationship between aromaticity and nitrogen bonding, which can signal molecular properties affecting biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: There may be non-linear dependencies between aromaticity and nitrogen bonds, which this feature aims to capture for better predictions.\n\n# Interaction between Mi (mean first ionization potential) and Psi_i_A (intrinsic state pseudoconnectivity index – type S average)\ndf['Mi_Psi_i_A_interaction'] = df['Mi'] * df['Psi_i_A']\n# Feature description: Combines electronic properties (mean ionization potential) with structural connectivity (pseudoconnectivity) index for better representation of molecular reactivity.\n# Usefulness: These combined aspects can hint towards molecular stability or reactivity, which are critical for understanding biodegradability.\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Apply statistical transformations to improve feature distributions\n# Z-score normalization on SM6_L (Spectral moment of order 6 from Laplace matrix)\ndf['zscore_SM6_L'] = (df['SM6_L'] - df['SM6_L'].mean()) / df['SM6_L'].std()\n# Feature description: Z-score normalization centers and scales SM6_L, which may have domain-specific variations being a molecular connectivity index.\n# Usefulness: Improves feature scaling and makes this feature more comparable with other descriptors, which can improve model efficiency.\n\n# Log transformation on nCIR (Number of circuits)\ndf['log_nCIR'] = np.log1p(df['nCIR'])\n# Feature description: Log transformation reduces skewness in the distribution of the number of circuits, which is likely highly right-skewed due to its range (0 to 147) and high variability.\n# Usefulness: Improves feature usability by reducing outliers’ effect and ensuring all values contribute meaningfully to the model.\n\n# Sine transformation on SpMax_A (Leading eigenvalue from adjacency matrix)\ndf['sine_SpMax_A'] = np.sin(df['SpMax_A'])\n# Feature description: Applies sine transformation to capture periodicity or oscillatory patterns in SpMax_A values, which might relate to molecular symmetry.\n# Usefulness: Such transformations can capture additional periodic patterns that may be related to molecular properties influencing biodegradability.\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Drop sparse or redundant features\n# Explanation why the column N-073 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['N-073'], inplace=True)\n# N-073: Ar2NH / Ar3N / Ar2N-Al / R..N..R is heavily sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0348, Std = 0.2223), contributing minimal information to the model.\n# Usefulness: Removing this feature reduces potential noise from non-informative sparse features, helping the model focus on meaningful signals.\n\n# Explanation why the column nArNO2 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nArNO2'], inplace=True)\n# nArNO2: Number of nitro groups (aromatic) is extremely sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0806, Std = 0.3302).\n# Usefulness: Eliminating this feature prevents overfitting and reduces dimensionality by removing non-contributory sparse data.\n\n# Explanation why the column B01[C-Br] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B01[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n# B01[C-Br]: Presence/absence of C-Br at topological distance 1 is redundant (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0348, Std = 0.1833).\n# Usefulness: Reducing redundancy simplifies the feature set and avoids adding noise due to overly localized and sparse data.\nend", "### Step 1: Generate new interaction features for better synergy exploration\nCreating interaction features helps capture hidden patterns and complex relationships between descriptors, which can significantly improve downstream performance.\n\npython\n# nO_Psi_i_A_interaction\ndf['nO_Psi_i_A_interaction'] = df['nO'] * df['Psi_i_A']\n# Feature description: Interaction of the number of oxygen atoms with the intrinsic pseudoconnectivity index captures the relationship between chemical composition and structural connectivity.\n# Usefulness: Highlights the synergy between oxygen-related attributes and molecular topological features relevant for biodegradability.\n\n# SdO_C_Percentage_ratio\ndf['SdO_C_Percentage_ratio'] = df['SdO'] / (df['C%'] + 1e-6)\n# Feature description: Ratio of the sum of dO E-states to carbon percentage measures the relative chemical contribution of oxygen compared to carbon.\n# Usefulness: Helps identify the chemical dominance of oxygen in a molecule, which could influence the molecule's readiness for biodegradability.\n\n# nCp_Me_interaction\ndf['nCp_Me_interaction'] = df['nCp'] * df['Me']\n# Feature description: Interaction between the number of terminal primary Carbon atoms and mean atomic Sanderson electronegativity reflects electronic and structural properties.\n# Usefulness: Captures the molecular reactivity and stability associated with carbon atoms, which play a critical role in biodegradation.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Apply targeted transformations for distribution normalization and outlier control\nNonlinear transformations help normalize feature distributions, reduce the impact of extreme values, and emphasize subtle patterns.\n\npython\n# log_F03[C-O]\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = np.log1p(df['F03[C-O]'])\n# Feature description: Log-transform of the frequency of C-O at topological distance 3 to reduce skewness and smooth out large variations.\n# Usefulness: Helps in emphasizing smaller values while controlling for extreme variations in carbon-oxygen bonding patterns.\n\n# sqrt_SpMax_B(m)\ndf['sqrt_SpMax_B(m)'] = np.sqrt(df['SpMax_B(m)'])\n# Feature description: Square root transformation of spectral properties reduces the effects of large values while capturing meaningful differences.\n# Usefulness: Enhances interpretability of spectral properties that can indirectly indicate molecular size or polarizability relevant to biodegradation.\n\n# rank_SdssC\ndf['rank_SdssC'] = df['SdssC'].rank()\n# Feature description: Converts SdssC into a rank-based feature to capture relative importance without being affected by scale or extreme values.\n# Usefulness: Rank transformations are useful when the overall order of values matters more than their exact magnitudes, reducing noise from outliers.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Drop sparse or redundant features\nSparse and redundant features provide minimal utility for biodegradability prediction while adding noise to the model.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column nArCOOR is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nArCOOR'], inplace=True)\n# nArCOOR: Number of aromatic esters is sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.0506, Std = 0.3039) and poorly represents molecular biodegradability.\n# Usefulness: Removing this feature helps simplify the dataset while focusing on more informative features.\n\n# Explanation why the column NssssC is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['NssssC'], inplace=True)\n# NssssC: Number of atoms of type ssssC is highly sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.3033, Std = 1.0950) with low relevance to biodegradability predictions.\n# Usefulness: Eliminates noise from a sparse and domain-irrelevant feature.\n\n# Explanation why the column B03[C-Cl] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B03[C-Cl]'], inplace=True)\n# B03[C-Cl]: Presence/absence of C-Cl at topological distance 3 is sparse (Q3 = 0, Mean = 0.1596, Std = 0.3665) and has minimal domain significance.\n# Usefulness: Reduces dataset dimensionality by dropping a sparse feature with limited predictive contribution.\nend"), with improvements -
